{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low bias: model forecasts are systematically below realized prices by ~1–4 EUR across most horizons, especially during uptrends. Apply an upward level shift to the whole path (larger when recent momentum is positive).",
    "Trend underreaction / lag: after a breakout (e.g., move from low-60s to high-60s), the model adjusts too slowly and stays anchored to prior range. Add a momentum term based on last 5–10 day slope to steepen the first 1–15 forecast days.",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: underprediction grows with horizon (days ~10–25 show the largest gaps in examples). Use a bias ramp that increases with lead time rather than a flat correction.",
    "Excess mean-reversion: forecasts revert toward mid-60s even when the realized path stays elevated (upper-60s/near-70). Reduce mean-reversion strength when price is above recent 20–30d average and the average is rising.",
    "Occasional late-horizon downside overshoot: in some cases the model projects a drop into low-60s at the end of the horizon while realized remains high (or rebounds). Cap late-horizon drawdowns unless confirmed by negative recent momentum/volatility regime shift."
  ]
}
